Nah, takes away the fun. See my first paragraph for balance considerations.

> Controlled polymorphs also ought to be limited in the ability to reach 
> for the biggest baddest beasts.  Say your percentage chance of a 
> controlled polymorph is equal to your current level divided by the 
> target difficulty (which can easily be much more than 100% for high 
> level characters) and if you fail this roll then roll for system shock 
> and apply a random polymorph by the rules above.

Most people don't polyself to get tougher, but to get certain special
abilities (eating certain materials, phasing, flying, stealing, etc.). It
uses consumable resources and requires a rare item (=oPC). I don't see an
issue there.

> So if you go polypetting in the mines then you should expect to get a 
> dwarf or gnome most of the time.

Effectively taking away that (rather interesting) feature from the game,
since starting pets are usually tougher than randomly generated monsters at
that stage.

